Hello,

My name is Mike Martin, and I am currently working on my M. Sc. I am=20
writing a research paper investigating the feasibility of developing an=20
API for a VPN, as discussed in the following quote:

=93It would be nice if the Grid application could access the VPN console,=20
re-affirm credentials, and register for notifications through an API=20
like the GSS or the Advance Reservation API listed above.=94
-Volker Sander (ed.) (2004) /Networking Issues for Grid Infrastructure/.=20
Global Grid Forum Document Series GFD-37. Available from:=20
http://www.ggf.org/documents/GFD.37.pdf

I started this knowing very little about VPNs, or about API design, but=20
my research has led me to believe that OpenVPN would be the best tool=20
for the job (I am a firm believer in Open Source Software). I am=20
wondering if any OpenVPN developers would be willing to fill out a short=20
(from my perspective) questionnaire regarding the requirements for such=20
an API for OpenVPN, either for Grid applications specifically, or for=20
any client application in general, as I have come to the conclusion that=20
an API should not be specific to the client application, but should be=20
as general as possible. Any help at all would be greatly appreciated.

If anyone is interested in participating, please email me off line so we=20
don't clutter your mailing list - unless there is such great interest=20
you would like to see it in the discussion.

I am a professor of Computer Studies at Seneca College in Toronto:=20
mike.martin@...777...

Thanks, you guys have created a great piece of software,

Mike

P.S. We are having our annual Open Source Software Conference in=20
October. Here is the url:
http://cs.senecac.on.ca/fsoss/2006/

It would be good to see OpenVPN people there. Next year I would like to=20
do a presentation about OpenVPN, because I am getting so tired of=20
everyone thinking IPSec is the only high quality VPN technology. They=20
all suffer from the same misconceptions about SSL VPNs that are=20
discussed by Charlie Hosner in his SANS paper. I feel like a prophet=20
lost in the wilderness.




